title: State of Multipathing on Linux
agents: linux
catalog: os/storage
license: GPLv2
distribution: check_mk
description:
 This check monitors the state of multipath devices on Linux
 hosts by comparing the number of active paths with the number
 of paths that was recorded when the service was discovered.

 Without a rule, the check goes {WARN} if fewer paths are active
 than recorded, for example because a path is broken or has
 disappeared from the multipath output entirely. It also goes
 {WARN} if more paths than recorded are present. A rediscovery
 updates the recorded number.

 With the ruleset "Linux and Solaris Multipath Count" you can
 instead configure an expected number of active paths, in which
 case the check goes {CRIT} if fewer paths are active and {WARN}
 if more, or lower levels for the percentage of active paths.

 Paths that are listed but not active are named in the service
 output. If the device has no paths at all, the check goes {CRIT}.

 This check is based on the output of the command {multipath -l} as
 sent by the Linux agent. It correctly deals with various subtle
 differences between the available versions of the multipath tools.

item:
 The UUID of the multipath device, e.g. {"360a980004334644d654a364469555a76"}.
 Via {inventory_multipath_rules} you can specify whether an alias should be used
 as item instead of the UUID. If the multipath device has an alias the alias will
 be used as item.

discovery:
 One service is created for each multipath device. The number of
 paths at that time is recorded with the service.
